C. MISMATCHES -- one entry each
------------------------------------------------------------------------
C1. THE COIL SHORT.  diagnosis = tooling (Magic cannot represent an inductor)
    STATUS: worked around, on both sides, with a hand-built artifact.

    Symptom: Magic extraction printed, twice,
        Warning:  Ports "B" and "A" are electrically shorted.
    and vco_spiral_ind.ext contained the line   equiv "B" "A".
    There is no `device` line for an inductor in sky130A.tech, so the drawn
    spiral extracts as plain wire and its two terminals become ONE node.

    This is worse than "LVS cannot match the coil on topology". The short
    PROPAGATES: each coil ties one of {voutp, voutn} to VDD, so the flat
    extraction collapsed VDD + voutp + voutn into a single net. The extracted
    top cell came out as
        .subckt routed VDD Vcont VSS Iref            <- 4 ports, not 6
        XXC0_0 VDD VDD XC0                           <- MiM cap, both plates one node
    Baseline netgen verdict (lvs_attempt_1/lvs.out):
        "Final result: Top level cell failed pin matching."
        voutn **Mismatch** / voutp **Mismatch**, "(no pin, node is VSS)" x2.

    The instructed schematic-side fix -- appending
        ignore class "-circuit1 vco_spiral_ind"
        ignore class "-circuit2 vco_spiral_ind"
    to a copy of sky130A_setup.tcl -- is NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT, and
    this is the single most important finding in this report. `ignore class`
    can only delete a subcircuit INSTANCE. On the layout side there is no
    instance to delete: ext2spice omits a subcircuit that contains no devices,
    and by then the A==B merge has already fused the parent's nets. The damage
    happens inside Magic, upstream of anything netgen can be told.

    What I tried, in order:
      1. `ignore class` alone (lvs_attempt_1)  -> FAILED. Pin matching failed,
         4 ports vs 6.
      2. `property LEFview true` on the coil cell before `extract all`
         (lvs_attempt_2_lefview) -> FAILED SILENTLY. Magic accepted the
         command without complaint, still extracted the coil internals, still
         printed "Ports B and A are electrically shorted", still emitted
         equiv "B" "A". Its only effect was to make ext2spice now emit
         `Xvco_spiral_ind_0 VDD VDD vco_spiral_ind` -- both terminals on the
         same node, i.e. the short made visible but not removed. Magic's
         documented abstract-view mechanism does not black-box extraction here.
      3. Hand-editing vco_spiral_ind.ext to delete `equiv "B" "A"` and add a
         real node "A", then re-running `ext2spice` alone against the edited
         .ext hierarchy (lvs_attempt_3) -> PARTIALLY WORKED, and is worth
         recording because it is a genuinely useful technique: `ext2spice`
         reads .ext files from disk and does NOT re-extract, so the edit
         survives. voutn came back (5 ports). But voutp did NOT: Magic had
         already written the PARENT's hierarchical connections against the
         merged canonical node, so both of coil 1's landings were recorded as
         `vco_spiral_ind_1/B` and port A got a stray unrelated connection.
         Splitting the child cannot undo a parent-child assignment that was
         made while the child was shorted.
      4. An EXTRACTION-ONLY GDS view (lvs_attempt_4) -> WORKED. In a COPY of
         the GDS I deleted exactly one polygon: the met5 (72/20) spiral of
         cell vco_spiral_ind, the single conductor that fuses A to B, and
         added back a 90,148 - 98,153 um met5 patch so port B's 72/16 pin
         still lands on real m5. Everything else -- both met4 landing
         structures, all 340 via4 cuts, both port labels -- is untouched, so
         both router landings still connect to the same terminals they
         physically connect to. Result: no short warning, two independent
         nodes A and B, 6 ports, and a unique match.
         View file : layout_fixer_work/lvs_attempt_4/routed_lvsview.gds
         DELIVERED GDS IS NOT THIS FILE and contains the full spiral.

    Where the real fix belongs (NOT layout, NOT mine): this is a
    tooling/PDK-integration issue for the orchestrator. Either
      (a) src/cells/primitives/inductor.py emits an abstract/blackbox companion
          view for extraction, or
      (b) the flow adds a sky130A.tech `device` entry (or an equivalent
          extraction-time cut) for the spiral cell,
    so the black box is produced by a script rather than by an agent editing a
    GDS copy by hand. As it stands, the LVS gate for this circuit class is not
    reproducible without manual intervention.

C2. "voutp on XC0 (bottom_met)" predicted DIRTY  -> FALSE ALARM, no short.
C3. "VDD on XC1 (top_met)" predicted DIRTY       -> FALSE ALARM, no short.
    routing_summary.txt predicted "expect an extraction-level short on this
    net" for both. layout-agent could not reproduce either geometrically and
    left the call to LVS. Settled: BOTH ARE FALSE ALARMS. Evidence, two
    independent ways:
      - geometric, per plate and per layer:
          XC0 met3 bottom plate (224.374,333.539)-(232.156,343.521)
              touched only by net voutp (2 segments, 0.494 um2)    CORRECT
          XC0 met4 top-plate contact (224.514,335.879)-(232.016,345.581)
              touched only by net VDD (1 segment, 0.101 um2)       CORRECT
          XC1 met3 bottom plate (236.889,280.184)-(244.671,290.166)
              touched only by net voutn                            CORRECT
          XC1 met4 top-plate contact (237.029,282.524)-(244.531,292.226)
              touched only by net VDD                              CORRECT
        No plate is touched by more than one net, on any layer.
      - extracted, in the final netlist:
          XXC0_0 voutp VDD XC0     XXC1_0 voutn VDD XC1
        matching the schematic's XC0 VDD voutp / XC1 VDD voutn exactly.
    Root cause of the false prediction: the router's corridor check flags a
    landing whose corridor crosses another net's metal on ANY layer of the
    macro box. On a MiM cap the plates are stacked by construction, so a
    legal landing on one plate always sits over the other -- exactly
    layout-agent's hypothesis, now confirmed. This is a router-side
    false-positive worth fixing in route_nets.py's corridor-clean check
    (it should exempt the other plate of the macro it is landing on);
    left as a source-fix note, not touched here.

    NOTE: I initially mis-attributed the coil short to XC0 while tracing,
    because a union-find over the .ext merge graph runs voutp -> XC0 plate ->
    coil -> XC0 plate -> VDD, and every edge on that path is a bridge. The
    per-plate/per-layer geometric check above is what disambiguated it. If
    this trap recurs: an .ext merge path is not evidence of a short at any
    particular point on the path.

C4. Resistor R-vs-X element-prefix quirk (environment.md): NOT ENCOUNTERED.
    Checked for it explicitly because the schematic's vco_spiral_ind body
    contains `R1 n1 B 9.2250`, which is the exact shape that triggers it. It
    never reached netgen as a device: the coil is black-boxed on both sides,
    so R1 is inside an ignored class. No dummy_N / proxy nets appear anywhere
    in the report. No wasted iterations on this one.

C5. Device with drain and source shorted (XN2/XN3, `Vcont ... Vcont`): matched
    with no special handling. Extracted as `XXN2_0 Vcont voutp Vcont VSS`.
    Worth recording as a negative result -- this was flagged as a risk for
    this circuit class and it simply was not one.

